    "slug": "90-day-ai-implementation-plan",
    "title": "A 90-Day AI Implementation Plan: Proof, System and Handover",
    "h1": "A 90-Day AI Implementation Plan: Proof, System and Handover",
    "description": "A practical AI rollout framework for Indian MSMEs: define a baseline, prove one task, build a repeatable workflow and hand over with measured evidence.",
    "date": "2026-06-13",
    "tags": [
      "AI Implementation",
      "Roadmap",
      "MSME"
    ],
    "originalHeadline": "A 90-Day AI Implementation Plan: Proof, System and Handover",
    "cover": null,
    "linkedinSlug": "he-texted-me-sir-paisa-waste-ho-gaya-umang-thakkar-tkh6c",
    "words": 888,
    "minutes": 5,
    "html": "<p><em>Editorial update: 14 November 2026. This version presents practical planning guidance; unverified numerical and client-outcome claims from the earlier version have been removed.</em></p><p>Buying an AI tool is a small part of introducing it into a business. Someone must choose the task, prepare the inputs, check the output and keep the workflow working after the initial setup.</p><p>The plan below is a proposed structure for that work. Ninety days is a planning horizon, not a promise of completion or financial return. Move through the gates when the evidence is ready. Some projects need longer; some should stop after a pilot.</p><p>The three gates are proof, system and handover. They help an Indian MSME decide what to test, what to document and when to expand.</p><h2>Before day one: define the baseline</h2><p>Choose one task with accessible inputs, a clear reviewer and a result you can inspect. A draft dispatch summary or an internal enquiry classification may be easier to contain than autonomous price negotiation. Suitability depends on your process and controls.</p><p>Write down the task&#x27;s volume, current completion time, correction effort and common exceptions. Define an accepted result before comparing manual and AI-assisted work.</p><p>Record the costs you will track, including preparation, review, software and support. Begin measuring from the pilot; waiting until the end makes it harder to establish what changed.</p><h2>Gate 1: Days 1–30 — prove one useful task</h2><h3>Start with a controlled scope</h3><p>Give the pilot only the access it needs. Use approved samples first, then supervised workplace inputs where appropriate. Keep a manual fallback available.</p><p>For a dispatch summary, require links to source records and a visible warning when production and dispatch figures do not reconcile. Producing a fluent paragraph is not enough. The reviewer must be able to explain where its numbers came from.</p><h3>Involve the process owner</h3><p>The person accountable for the work should inspect outputs and listen to the people doing the checking. The business owner can sponsor the project without having to operate every task personally.</p><p>Record mistakes, missing inputs and unclear instructions. Use them to improve the workflow rather than concealing them behind an average success rate.</p><h3>Proof gate checklist</h3><ul><li>The task and acceptance criteria are written down.</li><li>Representative outputs have been checked against their sources.</li><li>Review time, corrections and costs are recorded alongside processing time.</li><li>Exceptions reach a named person and a manual fallback is available.</li><li>The process owner has agreed whether to continue, revise or stop.</li></ul><p>Do not expand merely because a calendar milestone has arrived. If the pilot is not useful after review and correction, revisit the task or design.</p><h2>Gate 2: Days 31–60 — make the workflow repeatable</h2><h3>Maintain the business context</h3><p>Document the policies, product fields, terminology and instructions the workflow actually uses. Give each source an owner and a review date. Restrict access to the records needed for the task.</p><p>A maintained context pack can make the workflow easier to operate and update. It does not create a guaranteed competitive advantage or remove the need for testing.</p><h3>Let the team operate it</h3><p>Train the employees who will use the workflow on ordinary cases, missing information and conflicting inputs. Ask them to demonstrate how they check an output and escalate a problem without the implementer doing it for them.</p><p>Add another workflow only when the team has capacity to maintain it. There is no required number of automations or enthusiastic users that proves a system is ready.</p><h3>System gate checklist</h3><ul><li>Working instructions and approved context sources are available.</li><li>Team members can operate and check the agreed task.</li><li>Access, duplicate handling, failure alerts and escalation are tested.</li><li>A named owner maintains the workflow and its source information.</li><li>Any proposed expansion has its own scope and acceptance criteria.</li></ul><h2>Gate 3: Days 61–90 — hand over and review value</h2><h3>Compare like with like</h3><p>Compare accepted work against the baseline using the same task definition. Include human preparation, checking and rework. Record changes in volume or complexity that could explain a difference.</p><p>Separate hours released from cash expense avoided. If the team uses released time for other work, name that work. Do not count the same hours twice or claim that adoption alone proves a productivity gain.</p><h3>Practise the handover</h3><p>Ask the operating team to run the workflow, handle an exception and explain how to pause it. Agree who responds when an integration fails or a source document changes. Document maintenance responsibilities and support arrangements.</p><h3>Handover gate checklist</h3><ul><li>The team can operate the workflow and its fallback.</li><li>Costs, accepted outputs, corrections and time are reviewed together.</li><li>Known limitations and unresolved issues are documented.</li><li>Support, access and maintenance responsibilities are assigned.</li><li>The next decision is explicit: continue, improve, expand or retire.</li></ul><h2>What to do on Monday</h2><p>Bring the process owner and one person who performs the task into a short working session. Define the task, the source records, the reviewer and the evidence that would justify continuing. Identify a small pilot with manageable consequences.</p><p>The purpose of the plan is to make each decision inspectable. It does not guarantee a particular percentage improvement, hours saved or a successful rollout by day 90.</p><p>To scope a supervised pilot around your business process, explore <a href=\"https://bizgenix.ai/custom-solutions\">Bizgenix custom AI solutions</a>. For practical team development, explore <a href=\"https://bizgenix.ai/courses\">Bizgenix AI training</a>.</p>",
